.TH TPDUCK 1 "2026" "tpduck" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tpduck \- terminal TOTP/HOTP authenticator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tpduck
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B tpduck
.I command
.RI [ arguments ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tpduck
generates two-factor authentication codes in the terminal. Run without a
command it opens an interactive UI; with a command it behaves as an ordinary
CLI tool suitable for scripts.
.PP
Accounts live in a vault, by default
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/tpduck/accounts.json ,
written with mode 0600. The vault can be encrypted with a passphrase, after
which it is an authenticated envelope using Argon2id and XChaCha20-Poly1305.
.PP
A
.I query
naming an account is an exact ID, an exact
.IR Issuer:name " label,"
or any unique case-insensitive substring. An ambiguous query is refused rather
than guessed.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI code " query"
Print the current code. For a counter-based (HOTP) account this also advances
the counter, because such codes are single-use. With
.BR -peek
it changes nothing at all: the counter stays put, the use is not recorded, and
the vault is not written.
.TP
.BI verify " query code"
Report whether a code is one this account currently produces. Exits non-zero
when it is not.
.TP
.BI resync " query code"
Find the counter that produces a code and move an HOTP account just past it,
searching both directions.
.TP
.B list
List accounts. Never prints secrets.
.TP
.BI add " uri|file|image.png"
Import an
.IR otpauth:// " URI, an " otpauth-migration:// " export,"
a backup from another authenticator, or a PNG, JPEG or GIF screenshot
containing a QR code.
.TP
.B add \fB-secret\fI S \fB-name\fI N " [" \fB-issuer\fI I ...]
Add an account from its individual parts. The fields are
.BR -secret " (required), " -name " (required), " -issuer ", " -type
(totp, hotp or steam),
.BR -algo " (SHA1, SHA256 or SHA512), " -digits " (6 or 8), " -period ", "
.BR -counter " and " -notes .
Steam codes are always five characters, so
.B -digits
is ignored for them.
.TP
.BI rm " query"
Delete an account.
.TP
.BI pin " query"
Pin or unpin an account so it sorts to the top.
.TP
.BR order " " issuer | used
Order the list alphabetically or by most recently used.
.TP
.BI export " [query]"
Print
.I otpauth://
URIs. These contain secrets.
.TP
.BI qr " query"
Print a scannable QR code. It contains the secret.
.TP
.B migrate
Print
.I otpauth-migration://
QR codes for every account, readable by Google Authenticator's import. The
format has no Steam Guard, so Steam accounts are named on stderr and left out
rather than exported as a six-digit code Steam would reject; use
.B qr
for those.
.TP
.BI backup " file"
Write every account to a file, mode 0600 even if the file already existed with
something laxer. The format follows
.BR -format " (" uri ", the default, or " aegis " or " andotp ");"
.B -encrypt
seals the result with its own passphrase. Without it the file contains secrets
in plaintext.
.TP
.BI restore " file"
Import a backup. Aegis, andOTP, 2FAS, KeePassXC CSV and URI lists are
recognised automatically.
.TP
.BR encrypt ", " decrypt
Add or remove the vault passphrase. With
.B -yubikey
the vault is sealed to a YubiKey as well, so the file and the passphrase alone
will not open it; see
.B SEALING THE VAULT TO A YUBIKEY
below.
.B decrypt
removes both.
.TP
.BR keyring " " status | set | forget
Manage a passphrase stored in the operating system keyring.
.TP
.BR offset " " \fIseconds\fR | auto | none
Add a fixed offset to the clock when generating codes, for a device whose
clock cannot be synchronised. This masks a wrong clock rather than fixing it.
.TP
.B skew
Compare the system clock to network time over SNTP. This is the only command
that uses the network.
.TP
.BR yubikey " " list | code | put | delete | rename | password | reset
Use and manage OATH credentials stored on a YubiKey.
.B put
with a query takes the secret from the vault rather than from
.BR -secret ;
with
.B -move
it then deletes the vault's copy, but only after the key has reproduced a code
tpduck computed itself. A counter-based credential cannot be checked that way,
because asking it for a code advances the key's counter, so
.B -move
needs
.B -force
for one. Nothing can be copied back off a key: a credential there answers
challenges and never gives up its secret.
.B password
.RB ( set " or " remove )
protects the credentials on the key itself, and
.B reset
wipes the applet \- every credential and the password \- which cannot be undone
and destroys the only copy of each secret on it.
Requires a build with
.BR -tags " pcsc,"
which links libpcsclite; install it with
.I scripts/install-deps.sh --build
and build with
.IR "make build-pcsc" .
The
.I tpduck-pcsc
package provides such a build.
.TP
.B versions
List the retained copies of the vault. One is kept before every write that
changes the accounts \- adding, editing or deleting one, or changing a secret,
tag, note or pin. Taking a code also writes the vault, to record the use and
advance an HOTP counter, but does not rotate the copies, so ordinary use cannot
push an accidental deletion out of the history.
.TP
.BR undo " [" \fB-version\fI " n]"
Replace the vault with a retained copy. The restore is itself undoable.
.TP
.BI tag " [query tags]"
List the tags in use, or set a comma-separated list on an account.
.TP
.B profiles
List the named vaults. Select one with
.BR -p .
.TP
.BR agent " " run | status | stop | forget
Cache the derived key in memory so a run of commands does not re-derive it or
re-prompt. The lifetime is absolute, as with
.BR ssh-agent " " -t .
.TP
.BR completion " " bash | zsh | fish
Print a shell completion script.
.TP
.B version
Print the version, with the commit when the build was made in a checkout.
.TP
.B help
Print a summary of commands and flags.
.SH FLAGS
.TP
.BI -config " path"
Vault location.
.TP
.BI -idle-lock " duration"
Lock the UI after this much inactivity. 0 disables. Default 5m. It applies only
to an encrypted vault: a plaintext one has no key to discard and is readable on
disk anyway.
.TP
.BI -clip-clear " duration"
Clear a copied code from the clipboard after this long. 0 disables. Default 15s.
.TP
.B -keyring
Read the vault passphrase from the OS keyring.
.TP
.BI -window " n"
Periods either side of now to accept when verifying. Default 1.
.TP
.BI -batch " n"
Accounts per migration QR code. Default 10.
.TP
.BI -ntp " host:port"
Server for the clock check. Default pool.ntp.org:123.
.TP
.B -copy
With
.BR code ,
copy to the clipboard instead of printing.
.TP
.B -json
With
.BR list ,
produce machine-readable output.
.TP
.BI -p ", " -profile " name"
Use a named profile instead of the default vault.
.TP
.B -agent
Use a running agent to avoid re-deriving the key, for every command that opens
the vault. On by default; pass
.B -agent=false
to ignore one.
.TP
.BI -agent-ttl " duration"
How long the agent keeps a key. Default 15m, counted from when it was cached.
.TP
.BI -reader " name"
Substring of the smart-card reader to use for
.B yubikey
commands. Without it every reader is tried until one answers as an OATH applet.
.TP
.BI -version " n"
With
.BR undo ,
which retained copy to restore. 1 is the most recent.
.TP
.BI -format " fmt"
Backup and export format: uri, aegis or andotp.
.TP
.B -encrypt
With
.BR backup ,
seal the file with its own passphrase.
.TP
.BI -tags " a,b"
With
.BR add ,
tag the account; with
.BR list ,
filter by tag.
.TP
.B -touch
With
.BR "yubikey put" ,
require a physical touch to use the credential.
.TP
.B -peek
With
.BR code ,
read the code without consuming it: no counter is advanced, no use is recorded,
and the vault is not written.
.TP
.B -yubikey
With
.BR encrypt ,
seal the vault to a YubiKey's challenge-response slot as well as to the
passphrase.
.TP
.BI -slot " 1|2"
Which YubiKey challenge-response slot to use. Default 2, because slot 1 usually
still holds the factory Yubico OTP credential.
.TP
.B -move
With
.BR "yubikey put" ,
remove the vault's copy once the key has been shown to hold the credential.
.TP
.B -force
Do not ask for confirmation. Applies to
.BR rm ", " restore ", " undo ", " "yubikey delete" ", " "yubikey reset"
and an unverifiable
.BR "yubikey put -move" .
.SH INTERACTIVE KEYS
.TP
.BR Up / Down
Move between accounts.
.B Enter
copies the highlighted code.
.TP
.B /
Search.
.TP
.BR A ", " E ", " D
Add, edit, delete.
.TP
.BR I ", " X ", " M
Import, export, migration export.
.TP
.BR C ", " V ", " R
Copy, verify a code, reveal the secret.
.TP
.BR N ", " Y
Advance and resynchronise an HOTP counter.
.TP
.BR F ", " O
Pin an account, change the ordering.
.TP
.BR P ", " L ", " K
Passphrase, lock now (encrypted vaults only), clock check.
.TP
.BR ? ", " Q
Help, quit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TPDUCK_PASSPHRASE
Passphrase for an encrypted vault, used instead of prompting. Convenient for
scripts, but it exposes the passphrase to anything that can read the process
environment.
.TP
.B TPDUCK_YUBIKEY_PASSWORD
Password for a YubiKey's own OATH applet. It is a different secret from the
vault passphrase and has its own variable so that supplying one does not
silently supply the other.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Determines the default vault location.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Where
.B agent run
puts its socket.
.TP
.B TPDUCK_AGENT_SOCK
Overrides the agent socket path entirely. A Unix socket path is limited to
about 100 characters.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json
The vault.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json.lock
An advisory lock held while the vault is written.
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/accounts.json.1 \.\.\. .5
Retained copies of the previous vault, newest first. See
.BR versions " and " undo .
.TP
.I ~/.config/tpduck/profiles/NAME.json
A named profile's vault. Select one with
.BR -p .
.TP
.I $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/tpduck-agent.sock
The agent's socket, mode 0600. Without a runtime directory it goes in a 0700
directory of its own under the temporary directory instead.
.SH SEALING THE VAULT TO A YUBIKEY
.B tpduck encrypt -yubikey
derives the vault key from the passphrase
.I and
an HMAC-SHA1 challenge-response from the key, so a copy of the vault file is
not enough to open it: the slot's secret never leaves the key. An empty
passphrase makes the key the only factor, and the vault then opens with nothing
typed.
.PP
The slot must already be programmed for challenge-response.
.B tpduck
deliberately cannot programme one, because writing a slot is destructive and
belongs to
.BR ykman :
.PP
.RS
.B ykman otp chalresp --touch 2
.RE
.PP
Opening a sealed vault needs no flag: the vault records that it wants a key and
which slot answers, and tpduck asks for it. The derived key caches in the agent
like any other, so
.B tpduck agent run
means touching the key once rather than once per command. Changing the
passphrase keeps the binding;
.B tpduck decrypt
removes it.
.PP
If the key is lost the vault cannot be opened by anyone, including its owner.
Keep a
.B tpduck backup -encrypt
somewhere safe first: a backup is sealed with its own passphrase and never with
the key, so it survives losing one.
.SH THE CLIPBOARD
A copied code is cleared after
.BR -clip-clear ,
and only if the clipboard still holds what tpduck put there. Clearing alone is
not enough, because a clipboard history manager has already recorded the code,
so the copy is also marked as sensitive where the backend can say so:
.B wl-copy
passes
.BR --sensitive ,
which becomes the
.I x-kde-passwordManagerHint
type that Klipper and cliphist look for (wl-clipboard 2.3 or newer; older
versions are detected and the copy goes through unmarked), and on Windows
PowerShell sets the clipboard formats that keep a code out of the Win+V history
and out of cloud sync.
.PP
.BR xclip ", " xsel " and " pbcopy
cannot mark anything: an X11 selection owner advertises one target at a time,
and macOS's concealed type needs AppKit, which would end the pure-Go
cross-compile. tpduck reports which happened rather than letting you assume it
worked.
.SH SECURITY
A plaintext vault is readable by any process running as you; the UI says so
until a passphrase is set. Locking discards the decrypted accounts and zeroes
the derived key, but Go strings are immutable, so the secrets themselves cannot
be reliably scrubbed from process memory.
.PP
.BR export ", " qr ", " migrate " and " backup
all emit secrets.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on error, and 1 from
.B verify
when the code does not match or from
.B skew
when the clock is too far out.
.SH SEE ALSO
RFC 6238 (TOTP), RFC 4226 (HOTP), RFC 9106 (Argon2), RFC 2104 (HMAC).
.BR ykman (1)
programmes the challenge-response slot a sealed vault needs.
